1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,167
Can a parliament whose members wander in and out as they please still pass laws that never contradict one another?

2
00:00:07,467 --> 00:00:10,300
On the island of Paxos, it did.

3
00:00:14,466 --> 00:00:20,966
Take a group of computers that must all keep the same log of commands, in the same order.

4
00:00:21,266 --> 00:00:27,433
Any machine may stop and restart at any moment. Messages may be slow, lost, or delivered twice.

5
00:00:27,733 --> 00:00:35,566
In 1998 Leslie Lamport described Paxos: the logs never disagree, and when a majority is up and steady, new commands get through.

6
00:00:35,799 --> 00:00:41,633
He told it as the story of an ancient island parliament. We will follow the story.

7
00:00:43,333 --> 00:00:49,499
Paxos is shaped like a Y. Two arms reach north and face each other across a bay.

8
00:00:49,766 --> 00:00:58,266
The western arm holds the Chamber of the Paxon Parliament. The eastern arm holds the city of Schedia, which keeps laws of its own.

9
00:00:58,566 --> 00:01:06,399
Below the fork the stem widens into the island's southern coast. One road runs up the stem and out along both arms.

10
00:01:06,699 --> 00:01:14,533
The Chamber is a round hall of hard stone under one conical roof, with no podium and no head of the room.

11
00:01:14,799 --> 00:01:23,966
Its acoustics are poor, so oratory is impossible. Legislators can talk only through messengers, and the one door lets them come and go as they please.

12
00:01:25,666 --> 00:01:31,499
The Parliament's task is to settle the law of the land: a numbered sequence of decrees.

13
00:01:31,799 --> 00:01:41,632
Nobody will sit in the Chamber to keep the record, so each legislator keeps his own scroll, and writes into it the decrees that have passed, in order.

14
00:01:41,932 --> 00:01:50,432
First, consistency: no two scrolls may ever give different decrees for one number. A scroll may only lack an entry it hasn't heard of.

15
00:01:50,666 --> 00:01:59,499
Blank scrolls would be consistent too. So: progress. If a majority stays in the Chamber long enough, any proposed decree passes and is written down.

16
00:01:59,732 --> 00:02:07,566
It is hard. One group could pass a decree and leave for a banquet, and another could pass the opposite, knowing nothing.

17
00:02:09,532 --> 00:02:20,032
A legislator who leaves may forget what he was doing, so key notes go in the back of his scroll, in indelible ink. A slip of paper may be lost.

18
00:02:20,266 --> 00:02:27,766
Legislators can only send messengers. A messenger never garbles a message, but may forget he delivered it, and deliver it again.

19
00:02:27,999 --> 00:02:36,166
A messenger may leave on a long voyage and never deliver. Inside the Chamber everyone acts promptly; outside it, anything takes any time.

20
00:02:36,399 --> 00:02:40,899
And everyone is honest. The danger is not lies. It is absence.

21
00:02:42,999 --> 00:02:47,832
Everything rests on one fact: any two majorities of legislators share a member.

22
00:02:51,432 --> 00:02:57,265
That shared member remembers what the first majority decided, even when everyone else has wandered off.

23
00:02:57,565 --> 00:03:04,399
The Paxons first solved a smaller puzzle: a Synod of priests choosing one symbolic decree, once every nineteen years.

24
00:03:04,699 --> 00:03:13,532
A ballot is a vote on one decree by a chosen group, its quorum. It succeeds if every member of the quorum votes for it.

25
00:03:15,399 --> 00:03:19,899
Three conditions keep ballots safe. First, every ballot has its own number.

26
00:03:20,132 --> 00:03:22,965
Second, any two quorums share a priest.

27
00:03:23,232 --> 00:03:31,399
Third, if anyone in a ballot's quorum has voted before, the ballot must carry the decree of the latest of those earlier votes.

28
00:03:32,565 --> 00:03:40,399
Here are five ballots among five priests. A boxed priest is one who voted. Ballot 27's quorum is Α, Γ and Δ.

29
00:03:40,632 --> 00:03:49,132
Γ voted in ballot 5, and Δ in ballot 2. The latest is ballot 5, so ballot 27 must carry ballot 5's decree: β.

30
00:03:49,365 --> 00:03:55,865
Ballot 29 reaches back to ballot 27 in the same way. A decree, once chosen, is passed forward.

31
00:03:57,999 --> 00:04:06,499
A ballot is run by one priest, the initiator. He picks a fresh ballot number and a quorum, and sends each member a question.

32
00:04:06,732 --> 00:04:14,565
The question: tell me the latest vote you ever cast, and promise never to vote in any ballot numbered below this one.

33
00:04:14,799 --> 00:04:22,632
Each member who answers writes the promise in the back of his scroll, and replies with his latest vote, or with none.

34
00:04:22,899 --> 00:04:28,732
Now the initiator holds a fact about the past: the latest vote of everyone he asked.

35
00:04:30,699 --> 00:04:39,865
If anyone has voted before, he must propose the decree of the latest of those votes. Only if nobody ever voted is he free to choose.

36
00:04:40,099 --> 00:04:45,265
He sends the ballot to the quorum: vote for this decree, in this ballot.

37
00:04:45,499 --> 00:04:53,665
A member votes unless he has since promised something higher. His vote is written in the back of his scroll, and reported back.

38
00:04:53,932 --> 00:05:02,099
When every member of the quorum has voted, the ballot has succeeded. The initiator writes the decree in his scroll, and tells everyone.

39
00:05:02,332 --> 00:05:06,499
Anyone who hears writes it down. That is the whole ballot.

40
00:05:08,465 --> 00:05:14,965
A ballot is safe. But nothing yet makes anyone start one, and starting too many can block everything.

41
00:05:15,198 --> 00:05:23,365
A new ballot makes its quorum promise never to vote in anything lower, so each new ballot can doom the one before it.

42
00:05:23,598 --> 00:05:29,432
Two priests, each sure he is in charge, keep starting higher ballots, and neither ever finishes.

43
00:05:31,598 --> 00:05:40,765
The cure is to pick one president who alone starts ballots, and to start a new one only when the last has had time to succeed.

44
00:05:40,998 --> 00:05:44,832
Safety never depended on any of this. Only progress did.

45
00:05:47,531 --> 00:05:52,698
The real Parliament must pass a long series of decrees, numbered one after another.

46
00:05:52,931 --> 00:05:59,764
It is really a separate Synod for each decree number, but with a single president for all of them.

47
00:05:59,998 --> 00:06:07,498
A new president sends one question covering every open number at once. Each reply says what that legislator has voted for.

48
00:06:07,731 --> 00:06:17,564
Suppose he learns of decree 126 but nothing at all about 125. He fills the gap with a harmless decree: the ides of February is national olive day.

49
00:06:17,798 --> 00:06:23,298
Only then does he pass new decrees, in order after everything already in the book.

50
00:06:23,531 --> 00:06:31,364
Once a president is chosen, a decree needs just three steps: ask the quorum to vote, hear their votes, announce the result.

51
00:06:34,197 --> 00:06:38,031
Governing the island brought new problems, and the protocol grew.

52
00:06:38,264 --> 00:06:46,431
At first the president was whoever came first alphabetically, so a legislator back from months away had to copy six months of decrees.

53
00:06:46,664 --> 00:06:55,164
Scrolls of every decree grew too long, so they became law books holding only the current law and the number of the last decree.

54
00:06:55,397 --> 00:07:03,231
Legislators were added and removed by decree. And old decrees were passed again, so that a mistake in any scroll would heal.

55
00:07:04,797 --> 00:07:13,630
The island is a model of a replicated service: every server keeps the same log of commands, and the log is what the Parliament passes.

56
00:07:13,863 --> 00:07:22,697
The protocol never lets two logs disagree, however machines come and go. It makes progress whenever a majority is present and steady for long enough.

57
00:07:22,930 --> 00:07:32,097
This is how it lives with the limit we met on the hill: it never gives up safety, and asks for progress only once things settle.

58
00:07:32,330 --> 00:07:37,497
Its ballot protocol lives on as Paxos, the foundation of modern fault-tolerant distributed computing.
